  "quote_spans": [
    {
      "chunk_id": 24,
      "char_start": 561,
      "char_end": 957,
      "text": "In spite of the salt water and the rain, which he had to wipe off his\nface every minute, Salve went to his look-out post forward that night,\nand stood there humming to himself, whilst the rest of the crew who were\non duty slopped up and down on the deck-cargo below, in sea-boots and\ndripping oilskins, or sheltered themselves, as best they could, under\nthe lee of the round-house or forecastle"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 25,
      "char_start": 1556,
      "char_end": 1840,
      "text": "\" as\nthe sailors worked at the tough and heavy sail, with the cordage all\nstiff and swollen with ice and slippery with the rain, the spray driving\nin their faces, and the vessel rolling so that sometimes they were\nhanging on by the ropes only, when the deck went from under their feet"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 26,
      "char_start": 1025,
      "char_end": 1376,
      "text": "She was like many another\nvessel that is a good sailor enough, a little broken-backed from the\nweight of the cargo amidships; and as she gave to the strain, the ladder\nthat stood in the hold began to saw up and down in the coaming forward,\nwhile the water came oozing in through the staring bow timbers, and the\npumps had to be kept continually going"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 27,
      "char_start": 1118,
      "char_end": 1671,
      "text": "The gale showed no signs of moderating, and that night, as Salve\nKristiansen and another were taking their turn at the wheel, there\ngleamed suddenly out of the pitchy darkness to leeward of the\nfore-rigging the white crest of a tremendous eddy wave, which a moment\nafter came crashing down upon the deck, carrying clean away the\nround-house, binnacle, and long-boat, damaging the wheel, and leaving\nmany of the drenched and half--suffocated sailors deposited in the most\nunexpected places, and only glad to find that they still had the deck\nunder them"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 28,
      "char_start": 272,
      "char_end": 719,
      "text": "Salve had been too much occupied with the damaged wheel at first to have\na thought to spare for anything else; but it recurred to him very soon\nthat when that first dark sea had broken over them so unexpectedly from\nleeward, he had seen for a moment the glimmer of two lights on its\ncrest, and a world of associations was at once aroused in his mind: it\nseemed to the lad's romantic fancy that he was keeping an appointment\nwith Elizabeth Raklev"
    }
  ],
